sections.say:
"This is a string state-machine. The template has two modes — LITERAL (copy chars to output) and TOKEN (collect a key, then resolve it). I move forward through the string and switch modes when I hit `{{` or `}}`. That's exactly what `while + indexOf` gives me:

```
while (i < template.length) {
  const open = template.indexOf('{{', i);
  if (open === -1) { out += template.slice(i); break; }   // no more tokens — copy the tail
  out += template.slice(i, open);                          // copy the literal chunk before {{
  const close = template.indexOf('}}', open + 2);
  if (close === -1) { out += template.slice(open); break; }  // malformed — copy as literal, don't crash
  const key = template.slice(open + 2, close).trim();
  const val = key.split('.').reduce((o, k) => o == null ? undefined : o[k], data);
  out += val === undefined ? `[MISSING:${key}]` : String(val);
  i = close + 2;
}
```

"Three load-bearing pieces: (1) `indexOf` chases forward — cursor `i` only ever advances, so we visit every char exactly once. (2) `key.split('.').reduce(...)` walks dotted paths with a null-guard at each hop, so `client.name` doesn't crash if `client` is missing. (3) Missing → `[MISSING:${key}]`, never a throw. The next paralegal sees exactly which field broke."

